# a-crew-on-two-fronts — plan 14 H2, and what a ROSTER is worth.
#
# `a-base-on-two-fronts.keys` plus TWO lines: `crew 7 0` and
# `crew -7 0`.  Everything else is identical — same ground, same core,
# same towers, same walls, same waves.
#
#   nobody      (a-base-on-two-fronts):   77 ticks   ( 51 s)
#   one helper  (a-crew-on-one-front):   214 ticks   (143 s)
#   TWO helpers, both on the east:       214 ticks   — the second is
#                                                      worth NOTHING
#   this file — one on each front:       242 ticks   (161 s)
#
# ⚠⚠ **The third line is the phase.**  A second helper standing beside
# the first buys not one tick; the same helper standing on the other
# front buys 28.  So a crew's value is **COVERAGE — being in two
# places — and not throughput**, and this base can say why: one tower
# kills about eight robots over a couple of hundred ticks, which is
# 0.03 m of body a tick against a clearing rate of 0.33.  **One vehicle
# is already ten times faster than one front makes work.**
#
# That is `plans/14` § Goal in a number: *"the work the base needs
# doing stops being one vehicle's problem"* — because the work is in
# two places and a vehicle is in one.
#
# ── ⚠ The mechanism: both walls end BROKEN ──────────────────────
#
# Without a crew this base falls with both walls at 97 of 100 and a
# 1.5 m ramp on each front — the attackers never touched them.  With a
# helper on each front there is no ramp anywhere, and both walls are
# broken THROUGH: (5, 0), (5, 1), (-5, -1), (-5, 0) and (-5, 1) are all
# gone, and the hexes beside them read 30 rather than 100 because a
# perimeter unzips from a breach (plan 12 B3).
#
# ⚠ **So the crew adds no damage and takes 165 ticks off the enemy.**
# Every second of it comes from making them break a wall instead of
# walking up a staircase they built themselves — the same mechanism
# `a-crew-that-clears-up.keys` measures with the PLAYER in the kill
# zone, and it is worth the same whoever is driving.
#
# ⚠ The helpers are OUTSIDE the perimeter, and it is the same one-way
# commitment plan 13 V2 recorded: the ramp forms where the enemies die,
# a hover unit climbs 0.4 m, and a sealed base locks its own crew out
# as surely as it locks the player in.  `vehicle_boost` is the way back
# (plan 13 V4) and no helper has one — `helper.loft` gives them the
# chassis and not the boost, because nothing has needed to ask for it
# yet.

echo a crew on two fronts: two helpers, two denied ramps, two broken walls

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

do cycle_kind
click 3 0
marker 3 0 tower
click -3 0
marker -3 0 tower

do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 11 0
marker 11 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -11 0
marker -11 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -12 -2 12 -2
drag -12 -1 12 -1
drag -12 0 12 0
drag -12 1 12 1
drag -12 2 12 2
count painted 125 125

palette 9
click 5 -2
click 5 -1
click 5 0
click 5 1
click 5 2
click 5 -4
click 5 -3
click 5 3
click 5 4
click -5 -2
click -5 -1
click -5 0
click -5 1
click -5 2
click -5 -4
click -5 -3
click -5 3
click -5 4
kind 5 0 wall
kind -5 0 wall
hp 5 0 100.0 100.0
hp -5 0 100.0 100.0

# ⚠ **The whole difference from `a-base-on-two-fronts.keys`.**  One
# helper a front, each parked one hex clear of where its tower's kills
# will fall.  No key is pressed and none exists: clearing is a POSITION
# (`DESIGN.md` § 11), so what the player decides is where the crew
# STANDS.
crew 7 0
crew -7 0
helper 0 7 0
helper 1 -7 0

wave 10
count alive 10 10
wallet 200.0 200.0

tick 22
wave 16

fall 800
ticks 235 250
wallet 0.0 0.0

# ── What the crew changed ───────────────────────────────────────

# No ramp on either front.  Both towers built one all run and both
# helpers ate it as fast as it appeared.
pile 6 0 0.0 0.0
pile -6 0 0.0 0.0

# So they came through the walls instead — `wall` is gone from the
# middle of both, and what is left of each is an unzipping perimeter.
kind 5 0 grass
kind -5 0 grass
kind 5 -1 wall
hp 5 -1 25.0 35.0

# ⚠ The helpers never left the hexes they were placed on: a crew that
# is not ordered anywhere stays where the player put it, which is
# `plans/14` § Open questions 1 — helpers are passive and positional,
# and a dispatcher is where NPC AI quietly becomes pathfinding.
helper 0 7 0
helper 1 -7 0

snap h2_a_crew_on_two_fronts
